135
00:09:18,040 --> 00:09:21,080
That would satisfy our internal risk management team.

136
00:09:21,080 --> 00:09:25,080
Speaking of risk, payment terms are another key area.

137
00:09:25,080 --> 00:09:28,080
Your team prefers 90 days, correct?

138
00:09:28,080 --> 00:09:32,080
Yes, longer payment terms are essential for our cash flow planning.

139
00:09:32,080 --> 00:09:37,080
90 days is a challenge for us, but we can explore a staged approach.

140
00:09:37,080 --> 00:09:41,080
For example, 60 days for the first six months,

141
00:09:41,080 --> 00:09:44,080
moving to 90 days after demonstrated performance.

142
00:09:44,080 --> 00:09:48,080
That staged approach would be easier to gain internal approval.

143
00:09:48,080 --> 00:09:56,080
We could also include early payment incentives, allowing you to take advantage of small discounts if cash flow permits.

144
00:09:56,080 --> 00:09:58,080
That flexibility would be appreciated.

145
00:09:58,080 --> 00:10:02,080
Another area worth addressing is delivery reliability.

146
00:10:02,080 --> 00:10:07,080
We understand past delays with other suppliers have caused significant issues.

147
00:10:07,080 --> 00:10:11,080
Yes, consistent on-time delivery is a priority.

148
00:10:11,080 --> 00:10:20,080
We propose a 98% on-time delivery rate with defined escalation procedures and service credits for repeated failures.

149
00:10:20,080 --> 00:10:23,080
That would increase confidence in execution.

150
00:10:23,080 --> 00:10:30,080
Additionally, we can maintain buffer stock for critical items to prevent disruptions during peak periods.

151
00:10:30,080 --> 00:10:33,080
Who would carry the cost for the buffer stock?

152
00:10:33,080 --> 00:10:38,080
We would, provided we receive rolling forecasts with sufficient visibility.

153
00:10:38,080 --> 00:10:44,080
We can provide three-month rolling forecasts updated monthly, which should allow you to plan effectively.

154
00:10:44,080 --> 00:10:51,080
Perfect. Let's discuss quality compliance. Deviations directly impact our operations.

155
00:10:51,080 --> 00:10:56,080
Absolutely. Any compromise on quality is unacceptable.

156
00:10:56,080 --> 00:11:03,080
We propose joint quality audits twice a year and clear corrective action plans in case issues arise.

157
00:11:03,080 --> 00:11:06,080
That would provide the assurance management requires.

158
00:11:06,080 --> 00:11:14,360
In addition, we can establish KPIs for delivery performance, quality, responsiveness, and issue

159
00:11:14,360 --> 00:11:20,360
resolution with monthly operational reviews and quarterly management reviews.

160
00:11:20,360 --> 00:11:24,720
That structured approach aligns with our internal expectations.

161
00:11:24,720 --> 00:11:30,240
Incentives for exceptional performance could include priority handling, faster approvals,

162
00:11:30,240 --> 00:11:32,760
or additional commercial benefits.

163
00:11:32,760 --> 00:11:36,960
That creates motivation on both sides to maintain high standards.

164
00:11:36,960 --> 00:11:43,120
We'd also like to explore collaboration beyond operations, such as process improvements,

165
00:11:43,120 --> 00:11:47,040
supply chain optimization, and efficiency initiatives.

166
00:11:47,040 --> 00:11:53,560
That is appealing, particularly if it results in measurable savings or operational improvements.

167
00:11:53,560 --> 00:11:58,720
Periodic joint workshops could be used to identify and implement these improvements.

168
00:11:58,720 --> 00:12:02,240
That would also strengthen relations between our teams.

169
00:12:02,240 --> 00:12:07,480
During risk management, market volatility and unforeseen events need to be addressed

170
00:12:07,480 --> 00:12:08,800
