749
00:46:46,980 --> 00:46:51,860
Our goal today is to work through any issues openly and collaboratively.

750
00:46:51,860 --> 00:46:53,620
Where would you like to start?

751
00:46:53,620 --> 00:46:55,260
Let's begin with pricing.

752
00:46:55,260 --> 00:46:59,380
While we acknowledge the quality and service you offer, the overall cost is still higher

753
00:46:59,380 --> 00:47:01,820
than what our finance team is comfortable with.

754
00:47:01,820 --> 00:47:03,220
I understand.

755
00:47:03,220 --> 00:47:09,460
Our pricing reflects not just the product, but also reliability, service, and added value

756
00:47:09,460 --> 00:47:12,220
that often reduces hidden costs.

757
00:47:12,220 --> 00:47:16,820
But we're open to discussing adjustments to make the business case stronger.

758
00:47:16,820 --> 00:47:22,140
We recognize the added value, but internally it comes down to immediate budget impact.

759
00:47:22,140 --> 00:47:26,300
Our target is roughly a 10% reduction on the first year cost.

760
00:47:26,300 --> 00:47:31,420
One percent is ambitious, but perhaps we can explore a combination of price reduction and

761
00:47:31,420 --> 00:47:35,660
incentives tied to volume or long-term commitments.

762
00:47:35,660 --> 00:47:40,460
That approach could work, but we would need clarity on how those incentives are structured.

763
00:47:40,460 --> 00:47:41,460
Certainly.

764
00:47:41,460 --> 00:47:47,380
For example, we could offer a baseline discount of 6% with a further reduction if the volume

765
00:47:47,380 --> 00:47:50,060
exceeds a predefined target.

766
00:47:50,060 --> 00:47:53,020
What volume are you suggesting for the additional reduction?

767
00:47:53,020 --> 00:47:59,460
If monthly orders increase by 20% compared to the base forecast, we could add an extra

768
00:47:59,460 --> 00:48:01,460
3-4% discount.

769
00:48:01,460 --> 00:48:03,860
20% is a stretch initially.

770
00:48:03,860 --> 00:48:09,020
Would a phased approach work, starting with 10% growth and reviewing after three months?

771
00:48:09,020 --> 00:48:10,100
That's reasonable.

772
00:48:10,100 --> 00:48:16,100
We can structure it so that growth is monitored quarterly and the discount adjusts accordingly.

773
00:48:16,100 --> 00:48:18,940
That would be easier to justify to management.

774
00:48:18,940 --> 00:48:21,780
Another lever could be contract duration.

775
00:48:21,780 --> 00:48:24,780
agreements allow us to offer better pricing.

776
00:48:24,780 --> 00:48:27,220
Currently we're looking at a one-year arrangement.

777
00:48:27,220 --> 00:48:29,020
How much longer would you suggest?

778
00:48:29,020 --> 00:48:33,100
Ideally, a two-year term with annual review points.

779
00:48:33,100 --> 00:48:36,940
This provides stability and justifies stronger discounts.

780
00:48:36,940 --> 00:48:41,180
Two years is acceptable if there are clear exit clauses in case of underperformance

781
00:48:41,180 --> 00:48:42,940
or market changes.

782
00:48:42,940 --> 00:48:44,420
Absolutely.

783
00:48:44,420 --> 00:48:48,540
Termination clauses can be included based on performance metrics and significant

784
00:48:48,540 --> 00:48:50,120
market shifts.

785
00:48:50,120 --> 00:48:52,960
That would address our internal risk concerns.

786
00:48:52,960 --> 00:48:55,320
Let's also touch on payment terms.

787
00:48:55,320 --> 00:48:58,000
Your team prefers 90 days, correct?

788
00:48:58,000 --> 00:49:01,480
Yes, it's critical for our cash flow planning.

789
00:49:01,480 --> 00:49:06,680
90 days is challenging for us, but we could consider a staged approach, for example,

790
00:49:06,680 --> 00:49:13,000
60 days for the initial six months, moving to 90 days once volume stabilizes.

791
00:49:13,000 --> 00:49:16,240
That phased approach would make approval easier internally.

792
00:49:16,240 --> 00:49:21,780
To make it more flexible, we could offer early payment incentives, small discounts for settling

793
00:49:21,780 --> 00:49:24,300
invoices before the due date.

794
00:49:24,300 --> 00:49:28,380
That could work, giving us flexibility depending on cash availability.

795
00:49:28,380 --> 00:49:31,740
Another point we should cover is delivery reliability.

796
00:49:31,740 --> 00:49:35,660
We understand this has been an issue in the past with other suppliers.

797
00:49:35,660 --> 00:49:38,580
Yes, timely delivery is a priority.

798
00:49:38,580 --> 00:49:42,060
Delays have caused significant operational problems previously.

799
00:49:42,060 --> 00:49:49,620
To address that, we can commit to a 98% on-time delivery rate with defined escalation procedures.

800
00:49:49,620 --> 00:49:54,300
And if deliveries fall below that rate, we can introduce service credits for repeated

801
00:49:54,300 --> 00:49:57,140
failures ensuring accountability.

802
00:49:57,140 --> 00:49:59,060
That would increase our confidence.

803
00:49:59,060 --> 00:50:03,900
Additionally, we can maintain buffer stock to prevent disruptions during peak demand

804
00:50:03,900 --> 00:50:05,060
periods.

805
00:50:05,060 --> 00:50:07,540
Who would carry the cost for the buffer stock?

806
00:50:07,540 --> 00:50:12,620
We would, provided we receive reliable rolling forecasts from your side.

807
00:50:12,620 --> 00:50:16,220
We can provide three-month rolling forecasts updated monthly.

808
00:50:16,220 --> 00:50:18,180
That should give you enough visibility.

809
00:50:18,180 --> 00:50:19,180
Perfect.

810
00:50:19,180 --> 00:50:22,660
That helps us plan production and logistics efficiently.

811
00:50:22,660 --> 00:50:24,740
Another concern is quality compliance.

812
00:50:24,740 --> 00:50:28,460
Any deviation impacts our operations significantly.

813
00:50:28,460 --> 00:50:30,740
Quality is non-negotiable for us.

814
00:50:30,740 --> 00:50:36,020
We propose joint audits twice a year and clear corrective actions in case issues

815
00:50:36,020 --> 00:50:37,100
arise.

816
00:50:37,100 --> 00:50:39,100
That would provide the assurance we need.

817
00:50:39,100 --> 00:50:46,780
Additionally, we can include KPIs for delivery, quality, responsiveness, and issue resolution.

818
00:50:46,780 --> 00:50:51,500
Performance metrics would be reviewed monthly operationally and quarterly at management

819
00:50:51,500 --> 00:50:52,500
level.

820
00:50:52,500 --> 00:50:55,500
That structured approach aligns with our expectations.

821
00:50:55,500 --> 00:51:01,180
On top of that, we could discuss incentives for exceptional performance, priority handling,

822
00:51:01,180 --> 00:51:04,260
faster approvals, or commercial benefits.

823
00:51:04,260 --> 00:51:07,460
That would motivate both teams to maintain high standards.

824
00:51:07,460 --> 00:51:13,940
Beyond operations, we'd like to explore collaboration opportunities, such as joint process improvements

825
00:51:13,940 --> 00:51:16,540
or supply chain optimizations.

826
00:51:16,540 --> 00:51:21,060
That's appealing, especially if it leads to measurable savings or efficiency gains.

827
00:51:21,060 --> 00:51:25,820
We could set up periodic workshops to discuss innovations and improvements.

828
00:51:25,820 --> 00:51:29,180
That would also strengthen the relationship between our teams.

829
00:51:29,180 --> 00:51:31,660
Let's not forget risk management.

830
00:51:31,660 --> 00:51:36,100
Volatility and unforeseen events can impact both parties.

831
00:51:36,100 --> 00:51:38,620
How do you propose we mitigate these risks?

832
00:51:38,620 --> 00:51:44,900
Through clear contingency plans, predefined roles, communication protocols, and scenario

833
00:51:44,900 --> 00:51:49,980
planning, annual reviews would update the plan based on current conditions.

834
00:51:49,980 --> 00:51:53,540
That level of preparedness would be appreciated by management.

835
00:51:53,540 --> 00:51:56,580
Another operational topic is communication.

836
00:51:56,580 --> 00:52:00,260
Misalignment often stems from poor information flow.

837
00:52:00,260 --> 00:52:01,260
Agreed.

838
00:52:01,260 --> 00:52:07,220
Frequently would you propose formal communications, monthly operational calls, quarterly management

839
00:52:07,220 --> 00:52:11,260
reviews, and ad hoc escalation for urgent matters?

840
00:52:11,260 --> 00:52:13,260
That cadence would work well for us.

841
00:52:13,260 --> 00:52:18,940
Finally, we want to ensure clear transparency in all commercial and operational agreements

842
00:52:18,940 --> 00:52:20,940
to avoid misunderstandings.

843
00:52:20,940 --> 00:52:22,180
Absolutely.

844
00:52:22,180 --> 00:52:25,780
Clear documentation will be key to gaining internal approval.

845
00:52:25,780 --> 00:52:31,900
Based on our discussion, we can summarize tiered pricing with volume incentives, phased payment

846
00:52:31,900 --> 00:52:39,180
terms moving from 60 to 90 days, buffer stock support, service level agreements with credits,

847
00:52:39,180 --> 00:52:45,880
joint quality audits, KPIs with review cycles, and collaborative improvement initiatives.

848
00:52:45,880 --> 00:52:48,780
That summary accurately reflects our discussion.

849
00:52:48,780 --> 00:52:49,780
Excellent.

850
00:52:49,780 --> 00:52:55,480
Our next step is to draft a revised contract incorporating these points for your review.

851
00:52:55,480 --> 00:53:00,240
Once we receive it, we'll circulate it internally for approval, aiming to finalize within two

852
00:53:00,240 --> 00:53:01,240
weeks.

853
00:53:01,240 --> 00:53:03,120
That timeline works for us.

854
00:53:03,120 --> 00:53:06,080
Thank you for the constructive and transparent approach.

855
00:53:06,080 --> 00:53:07,280
Thank you as well.

856
00:53:07,280 --> 00:53:11,600
We're optimistic about building a long-term, mutually beneficial partnership.

857
00:53:11,600 --> 00:53:12,600
Agreed.

858
00:53:12,600 --> 00:53:15,120
Let's move forward with the draft and take it to the next stage.
